    "title": "Somalia's Grade 12 National Exam Sees Record 43,028 Students",
    "original_title": "Marxaladihii uu soo Maray Imtixaanka Qaranka ee Fasalka 12aad",
    "slug": "somalia-s-grade-12-national-exam-sees-record-43-028-students",
    "summary": "A record 43,028 students across Somalia, excluding Puntland and Somaliland, sat for the Grade 12 national exam, which opened at 158 centers in 58 districts. The exam, administered by the Ministry of Education for the twelfth consecutive year, marks a significant milestone in student participation.",
    "content": "Somalia's Federal Ministry of Education, Culture, and Higher Education announced that a record 43,028 students registered for the 2025/2026 Grade 12 national examination, the highest number since the exam's inception. The exams commenced this week across 158 centers spread over 58 districts in the Banadir region and other federal member states, excluding Puntland and Somaliland, which administer their own exams.\n\nThis year's figure represents a notable increase over previous years, reflecting the ministry's efforts to expand educational access. The exam, which is now in its twelfth consecutive national administration, serves as a primary benchmark for secondary school completion and university admission. According to an official from the ministry, the high turnout signals improved retention in the final year of secondary education, especially in areas that have historically struggled with low enrollment.\n\nThe exams are being conducted simultaneously across all centers to ensure uniformity and minimize leakage of questions. Security measures have been reinforced at testing sites, and local authorities have been tasked with ensuring a smooth process. The ministry has also established a hotline for students and parents to report any irregularities.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this milestone is particularly significant. Many diaspora families with ties to Somalia will be following these results closely, as they often support relatives' education back home. The increased participation suggests a growing confidence in the national education system, which has faced challenges ranging from resource constraints to insecurity. Moreover, diaspora members frequently sponsor students or collaborate with schools, and a larger cohort taking the exam means more young Somalis may be eligible for scholarships or university placements, both domestically and abroad.\n\nThe ministry expects to release results within two to three months, after which successful candidates will be eligible for admission to universities and other tertiary institutions. The education system's progress, evidenced by this record turnout, is a positive step toward rebuilding human capital in Somalia, a priority for many in the diaspora who seek to invest in the country's future.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/15/marxaladihii-uu-soo-maray-imtixaanka-qaranka-ee-fasalka-12aad/",
